course: dictionary
lesson: 11409
title: Money and benefits that build up
level: primary-6
stars: 2
explain: Accrue is a formal word. It means to grow or build up little by little over time. Think of a snowball rolling down a hill: it gets bigger a bit at a time, and that is how accrue works.
explain: «Interest accrues on my savings every year.» Interest is extra money a bank adds to your savings. The sentence tells you the total grows bit by bit as the years pass, so accrues must mean builds up slowly.
explain: Accrue can also mean good things coming to someone slowly over time. «Good habits accrue benefits: you feel fitter each month.» Check your meaning by swapping in build up: "Good habits build up benefits" still makes sense, so your meaning is right.
fact: Accrue is a verb: accrue, accrues, accrued, accruing.
fact: «Accrue» means to grow or build up little by little over time, especially money.
fact: «Interest accrues» means the money in an account grows as time goes on.
fact: «Accrued interest» means interest that has built up but has not been paid out yet.
fact: «Benefits accrue to» someone means good things come to them slowly over time.
exercise: «Interest accrues on the money in my savings account.» What does accrues mean here? | Stops growing when the bank closes | Is taken away all at once | *Grows little by little over time | Is spent on sweets today
exercise: «Good habits accrue benefits over time.» What does accrue mean? | *Build up slowly over time | Stop happening suddenly | Are forgotten the next day | Break down quickly
exercise: «Interest accrues each year, so the savings grow.» Find and copy the word that means build up slowly. | savings | year | *accrues | each
test: What does «accrued interest» mean? | Money you give away for free | *Interest that has built up over time but is not yet paid out | A type of bank card | Interest paid only on the first day
test: Mia says accrue is a word only for maths. What is the best reply? | It is only used when talking about gardens | It means to spend money quickly | *It is mostly used with money, but it can also mean benefits that come slowly over time | It means exactly the same as drop
exam: «Over the years, benefits accrue to people who keep practising.» What does accrue mean? | Happen only once | Stop suddenly | Disappear completely | *Build up slowly over time
